## PART C — ANNEXURE IDENTITY: the Talc case, and every future one like it

**The failing document:** `Regulatory_Talc 11.2023 VQ.pdf`, an IMI Fabi S.p.A. multi-topic regulatory letter, 11 pages.

**Its structure is the problem:**
- **Page 1** is headed *"DECLARATION OF COMPLIANCE – IMI Fabi Talc 140 USP-EP-ChP"* and is entirely about REACH/SVHC/ISO-16128 naturality. **No elemental-impurity content at all.**
- **Page 4** carries the *"ELEMENTAL IMPURITIES"* heading and the actual ICH Q3D table, whose own column header reads **"Mineral / Talc 140 USP-EP-ChP"** — no "IMI Fabi" prefix.
- Real values in that table: As 0.1 ppm, Cd <0.01, Hg <0.005, Pb <0.5, Co <0.5, Ni <1.0, V 5.0 ppm; Cr flagged likely-present with no value; all Class 2B and remaining Class 3 marked not present.
- **Page 5** separately declares the product free of residual solvents (relevant to RS, not EI).

**Two distinct failure modes, both must be fixed:**

**C1 — identity is grade/brand-qualified; the composition names the generic material.** The composition lists **"Talc"**. The annexure identifies as **"IMI Fabi Talc 140 USP-EP-ChP"** — a brand (`IMI Fabi`), a grade number (`140`), and three compendial suffixes (`USP-EP-ChP`). `BRAND_IDENTITY_RULES` has no `"imi fabi"` entry, so Stage 1 never fires, and canonical-core matching cannot reduce that string to the bare token `talc`.

**This is the same class as the CEOLUS UF711 case.** Do NOT fix it by adding `"imi fabi"` to the brand dictionary — that fixes one document and guarantees the next unlisted brand repeats it.

**Fix — generic, data-driven, no dictionary:**
- **Compendial-suffix stripping.** Before canonical-core comparison, strip trailing pharmacopoeia tokens (`USP`, `NF`, `EP`, `BP`, `JP`, `ChP`, `Ph. Eur.`, `PhEur`, `USP-NF`) and their hyphenated combinations from BOTH sides. `"IMI Fabi Talc 140 USP-EP-ChP"` → `"imi fabi talc 140"`, which now contains `talc` as a whole token.
- **Grade-number tolerance.** A trailing bare numeric token (`140`, `200`, `711`) is a grade identifier, not part of the material name. It must not block containment when the other side omits it.
- **Stated-grade match (the CEOLUS fix, generalised).** If the composition ingredient's own parenthetical/bracketed text matches a candidate annexure's own `material_name` / `material_name_candidates` by exact fragment or canonical-core containment, that candidate wins at Stage-1 confidence. No brand dictionary required — it compares what the composition says against what the annexure says.
- **Letter–digit boundary normalisation.** Insert a token boundary at every letter→digit and digit→letter transition before tokenising, so `UF711` and `UF-711` both normalise to `{uf, 711}`. Apply identically to both sides. Verify this does NOT collapse genuinely distinct grades (`aerosil 200` vs `aerosil 200 vv` must stay separate).

**C2 — identity page and data page are different pages.** Page 1 names the material; page 4 holds the table. The metadata call reads page 1 only, and page 1 here is a compliance-letter title, not an EI document. This is the **same shape as the Propyl Gallate fix already shipped** (identity on the last page, data on the first) — but in the opposite direction, and the existing fallback only triggers when the page-1 name is missing/generic/boilerplate. Here page 1 DOES yield a plausible name, so the fallback never fires, and the name it yields is brand-qualified in a way C1 must then rescue.

**Fix — extend, do not replace, the existing `_page_identity_fallback`:**
- Collect candidate material identities from **every** page, not only page 1 and not only when page 1 failed. Keep page 1's name as primary, but add every other page's stated identity to `material_name_candidates`. The page-4 header *"Mineral / Talc 140 USP-EP-ChP"* then becomes a matchable candidate alongside page 1's *"IMI Fabi Talc 140 USP-EP-ChP"*.
- Do not require the identity-bearing page to contain element rows, and do not require the data-bearing page to restate the identity.
- **Preserve current behaviour exactly** when page 1 already yields a good name AND matching already succeeds — this must be purely additive candidate enrichment.

**C3 — make multi-topic documents generally safe.** This annexure covers REACH, SVHC, ISO-16128, Halal, CITES, nanomaterials, CMR, INCI, shelf-life, **elemental impurities**, **residual solvents**, Prop-65, COSMOS, VOC, microplastics, nitrosamines, azoxyalkyl, allergens. Future vendor letters will look like this. Ensure the EI extractor anchors on the *"ELEMENTAL IMPURITIES"* section heading rather than assuming a single-topic document, and that the RS extractor anchors on *"RESIDUAL SOLVENT"* — neither should be confused by the many other numeric tables (CAS numbers, EC numbers, EINECS refs, allergen lists) present in the same file.

**Before coding C1/C2, request the Diazoxide run's log** — specifically the `Identity validation for 'Talc'` warning and the POST-CLUSTER dump. That will confirm whether it is failing at identity resolution (C1) or at extraction/metadata (C2), because the two fixes are independent and you should know which is actually firing.

